{
  "concept": "Bellman-Ford computes single-source shortest paths by repeatedly relaxing every edge in a fixed order, letting distance estimates ratchet downward — even across negative-weight edges — until no estimate can improve.",
  "figureType": "network_diagram",
  "elements": [
    "Five vertex circles (light gray fill, black outline) labeled outside with italic letters: s at far left, t upper-center, y lower-center, x upper-right, z lower-right",
    "Current distance estimate d[v] drawn inside each circle (0, ∞, or a number; ∞ shown as the infinity glyph)",
    "Directed edge s→t, weight 6 (upper-left diagonal)",
    "Directed edge s→y, weight 7 (lower-left diagonal)",
    "Directed edge t→x, weight 5 (curved arc over the top, left to right)",
    "Directed edge x→t, weight −2 (curved arc just under it, right to left)",
    "Directed edge t→y, weight 8 (vertical, t down to y)",
    "Directed edge t→z, weight −4 (long diagonal, t down-right to z)",
    "Directed edge y→x, weight −3 (long diagonal, y up-right to x)",
    "Directed edge y→z, weight 9 (horizontal, bottom)",
    "Directed edge z→x, weight 7 (vertical, z up to x)",
    "Directed edge z→s, weight 2 (long horizontal-ish arrow back to s)",
    "Weight labels placed beside each edge (6, 7, 5, −2, 8, −4, −3, 9, 7, 2)",
    "Thick pale-gray highlight stroke drawn underneath edges that were successfully relaxed / belong to the current predecessor tree",
    "Panel captions (a)–(e) in the static reference view",
    "Pass counter / status readout: current pass number and number of distances changed in that pass",
    "Edge-relaxation order list with the currently relaxed edge marked"
  ],
  "interactions": [
    {
      "id": "relaxEditor",
      "type": "code_editor",
      "teaches": "That the entire algorithm is one tiny test — 'is going through u better than what I already know about v?' — applied to every edge, every pass; getting the comparison wrong changes exactly which edges shade gray and where the distances stall.",
      "label": "Implement relax(u, v, w)",
      "entry_point": "relax(u, v, w)",
      "api": [
        { "op": "getDist(v)", "does": "current estimate d[v] (Infinity if v not yet reached)" },
        { "op": "setDist(v, value)", "does": "the ONLY way to change an estimate; animates the number inside v's circle" },
        { "op": "setPred(v, u)", "does": "record u as v's predecessor; shades edge u→v gray in the shortest-path tree" },
        { "op": "clearPred(v)", "does": "drop v's predecessor edge from the tree highlight" },
        { "op": "note(text)", "does": "attach the learner's own comment to this frame of the replay" }
      ],
      "frame": "the whole 5-vertex graph: all d[v] labels, the edge (u,v) currently under test drawn in accent color, gray shading on every tree edge, plus pass number and change-count",
      "input": {
        "label": "Edge relaxation order (u,v,w)",
        "default": "t,x,5; t,y,8; t,z,-4; x,t,-2; y,x,-3; y,z,9; z,x,7; z,s,2; s,t,6; s,y,7"
      },
      "sample_code": "function relax(u, v, w) {\n  // can we reach v more cheaply by going through u?\n  if (getDist(u) + w < getDist(v)) {\n    setDist(v, getDist(u) + w);\n    setPred(v, u);\n    note(v + ' improved to ' + getDist(v) + ' via ' + u);\n  }\n}",
      "buggy_code": "function relax(u, v, w) {\n  // BUG: compares the edge weight to d[v] instead of d[u] + w,\n  // so paths never accumulate and negative edges are mis-handled\n  if (w < getDist(v)) {\n    setDist(v, w);\n    setPred(v, u);\n  }\n}",
      "success_check": "After the 4 passes, no edge (u,v,w) satisfies dist[u] + w < dist[v], and dist = {s:0, t:2, x:4, y:7, z:-2} with the tree s→y→x→t→z"
    },
    {
      "id": "pass",
      "type": "button",
      "teaches": "That each panel of the figure is one complete sweep over all ten edges, and that improvements shrink pass by pass until a whole pass changes nothing.",
      "label": "Relaxation pass",
      "states": ["init", "pass1", "pass2", "pass3", "pass4"],
      "default": "init"
    }
  ],
  "demo_steps": [
    {
      "title": "Initialization (panel a)",
      "narration": "Only the source knows anything: d[s] = 0 and every other estimate is ∞. All ten edges are thin black — nothing has been relaxed yet.",
      "state": { "pass": "init" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pass 1 — the source reaches its neighbours (panel b)",
      "narration": "Most edges leave vertices that are still ∞, so they do nothing; only s→t and s→y fire, giving d[t] = 6 and d[y] = 7. Two distances changed.",
      "state": { "pass": "pass1" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pass 2 — information spreads one hop further (panel c)",
      "narration": "y→x pulls x down to 4 (7 − 3) and t→z sets z = 2. Notice x was briefly 11 via t→x before y→x improved it — estimates only ever decrease.",
      "state": { "pass": "pass2" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pass 3 — a negative edge rewrites an earlier answer (panel d)",
      "narration": "The negative edge x→t relaxes t from 6 down to 2, so a vertex that looked settled two passes ago improves. Only one distance changes.",
      "state": { "pass": "pass3" }
    },
    {
      "title": "Pass 4 — convergence (panel e)",
      "narration": "The cheaper t propagates through t→z, dropping z to −2. The final tree is s→y→x→t→z; one more pass would change nothing, which is Bellman-Ford's stopping guarantee after |V| − 1 sweeps.",
      "state": { "pass": "pass4" }
    }
  ],
  "keyInsight": "Relaxation is monotone and local: every estimate can only fall, so sweeping all edges |V| − 1 times is enough for the true shortest-path values to ripple outward one hop per pass — even when a negative edge forces an already-'known' vertex to be corrected.",
  "reconstructionNotes": "Single interactive graph (not five side-by-side panels) reproducing the CLRS layout: s at far left mid-height; t above-center and y below-center; x upper-right and z lower-right, forming a wide diamond with a crossing X between {t,y} and {x,z}. Vertices are light-gray filled circles with black outlines, italic external labels (s, t, x, y, z), and the current d[v] centered inside (∞ glyph when unreached). Ten thin black arrows with arrowheads and small serif weight labels: s→t 6, s→y 7, t→x 5 (curved arc above), x→t −2 (curved arc just below it, pointing back), t→y 8 (vertical), t→z −4 and y→x −3 (the long crossing diagonals), y→z 9 (bottom horizontal), z→x 7 (right vertical), z→s 2 (long arrow back left). Relaxed / tree edges get a thick pale-gray stroke drawn behind the black arrow, exactly as in the book. The edge currently being tested by relax() is drawn in a single accent color (e.g. blue) with its weight label emphasized; a distance label animates (brief flash) when setDist changes it. Below or beside the graph: the code editor for relax(u,v,w), the ordered edge list with the current edge marked, a pass indicator (Init / Pass 1–4), a 'distances changed this pass' counter (2, 2, 1, 1, 0), and a small static thumbnail strip of panels (a)–(e) so the learner can match the live state to the textbook figure."
}

